767
00:41:44,681 --> 00:41:50,160
from places they're not wanted, like backyard swimming pools

768
00:41:50,161 --> 00:41:51,920
to places they are.

769
00:41:51,921 --> 00:41:54,260
Small gators are taken to a preserve,

770
00:41:54,261 --> 00:41:55,960
where they'll live out their lives.

771
00:41:55,961 --> 00:41:58,600
But the big ones, like this 9-foot male,

772
00:41:58,601 --> 00:42:01,760
accompany Paul and Jimmy to their other amazing job,

773
00:42:01,761 --> 00:42:02,960
alligator wrestling.

774
00:42:02,961 --> 00:42:04,360
Don't you know we're saving your life?

775
00:42:05,640 --> 00:42:15,820
It's a showcase of trapping techniques that originated with Florida's Seminole Indians.

776
00:42:15,821 --> 00:42:22,880
This pit is where Paul and Jimmy sharpen up their trapping skills.

777
00:42:22,881 --> 00:42:36,620
But nobody's perfect.

778
00:42:37,400 --> 00:42:43,020
I've been bit 19 times where a gator has actually locked on or bit and released or got a couple of teeth in me.

779
00:42:43,120 --> 00:42:44,880
I've had a 12-foot gator lock onto my hand.

780
00:42:46,740 --> 00:42:49,140
I've been grazed probably another 10 or 15 times.

781
00:42:49,400 --> 00:42:51,540
Put one tooth and rip a hole in my arm about that big.

782
00:42:51,541 --> 00:42:54,540
But, I mean, that's not that bad.

783
00:42:57,540 --> 00:42:59,540
Despite the risks, Paul and Jimmy

784
00:42:59,541 --> 00:43:02,180
use their unconventional gator wrestling skills

785
00:43:02,181 --> 00:43:05,180
in their trapping work.

786
00:43:05,181 --> 00:43:07,180
That's the principal reason we chose them

787
00:43:07,181 --> 00:43:09,060
as our crowning jaw holders.

788
00:43:09,061 --> 00:43:10,900
There's a certain dance you can do with him

789
00:43:10,901 --> 00:43:12,900
where you get on kind of his perimeter,

790
00:43:12,901 --> 00:43:14,300
and you kind of get a feel for him.

791
00:43:14,301 --> 00:43:16,060
You try to levitate him by picking up his jaw,

792
00:43:16,061 --> 00:43:17,340
and he'll back away.

793
00:43:17,341 --> 00:43:18,940
And do it again, he'll back away.

794
00:43:18,941 --> 00:43:21,060
But if you're gonna pull him out of a pool or something,

795
00:43:21,061 --> 00:43:23,060
you want to get him in a position where what I like to do

796
00:43:23,061 --> 00:43:27,060
is get him over my head and get my head underneath his front leg.

797
00:43:27,061 --> 00:43:30,060
And then once I get him there, I got to grab him and lock up.

798
00:43:30,061 --> 00:43:32,060
But you got to go quick because in that little bit of time

799
00:43:32,061 --> 00:43:34,060
where you're sliding your hands over,

800
00:43:34,061 --> 00:43:37,060
if he decides to come back, he can spin and bite your head off.

801
00:43:39,060 --> 00:43:41,060
That sobering possibility may be our best reason

802
00:43:41,061 --> 00:43:44,060
why these Mavericks merit the ultimate spot in our countdown.

803
00:43:46,060 --> 00:43:50,060
Their job and all the amazing jobs we've presented over the past hour

804
00:43:50,061 --> 00:43:53,800
past hour showcase the incredible spectrum of talent

805
00:43:53,801 --> 00:43:57,500
and ingenuity of America's workforce.

806
00:43:57,501 --> 00:44:00,100
These working stiffs show how it's deep within us

807
00:44:00,101 --> 00:44:01,900
to push the limits.

808
00:44:01,901 --> 00:44:05,500
And how earning a living says a lot about who we are

809
00:44:05,501 --> 00:44:07,620
and what we're made of.

